ASSIGNMENT 5
Instructions for Students
● Create a separate HTML and CSS file for each question.
● Do not use JavaScript or any external framework.
● Focus on semantic HTML and clean, well-indented CSS.
● Use comments in your CSS to explain the logic where necessary.
1. Create a personal portfolio homepage using HTML and CSS.
Requirements:
● The header should contain your name and navigation links (Home, About, Contact).
● Use position: fixed for the header so it stays at the top when scrolling.
● The main section should have a short bio paragraph and a profile image.
● Use different CSS units (px, em, %, vh, rem) across elements.
● Use custom background color and text color combinations that maintain contrast.
● The footer should always stay at the bottom of the page (use flexbox or positioning).
2. Design a multi-section informational webpage (for example: “About a City” or “Favorite
Destination”).
Requirements:
● Include a <header>, <nav>, <main>, and <footer>.
● Each section in <main> should have a full-width background color or image.
● Apply a linear gradient in one section and a radial gradient in another.
● Use padding, margins, and border to create spacing and define clear sections.
● Headings must use different font sizes and weights using CSS.
● At least two sections must demonstrate background-attachment: fixed; (parallax-like
effect).
3. Build a 3-column “Team Members” layout showing profile cards for three people.
Requirements:
● Use display: flex or display: grid to align cards in one row.
● Each card should contain a name, role, and short description.
● Use the Box Model to create uniform padding and margins.
● Add borders, rounded corners, and shadow effects to each card.
● Highlight one card (e.g., Team Lead) with a different background color and border style.
● The layout must automatically adjust to a single column when viewed on a screen
smaller than 600px (responsive layout).
4. Create a long article webpage with the following structure:
● Header → Article Title
● Subheading → Author name and publication date
● Body → At least 5 paragraphs of dummy text
Use CSS to:
● Add consistent spacing between paragraphs using margins.
● Style the first letter of each paragraph with a larger font using ::first-letter.
● Make the first line of each paragraph bold using ::first-line.
● Highlight key words using <mark> and style it with custom background color.
● Add a left border and background color to emphasize the blockquote section.
5. Design a pricing table with three pricing plans: Basic, Standard, and Premium.
Requirements:
● Use flexbox or grid to create three equal columns.
● Add a hover effect on each plan that slightly enlarges the box and changes background
color.
● Use CSS transitions for smooth hover animations.
● Each plan should have:
○ A heading (plan name)
○ A list of features
○ A price section
○ A button (styled link using <a>).
● Use different border styles or colors to highlight the most popular plan.
● All boxes should maintain equal height even when text varies.
6. Create a CSS-only photo gallery layout with at least 6 images.
Requirements:
● Use flexbox or grid to display images in multiple columns.
● When hovered, each image should:
○ Slightly scale up (transform: scale(1.1))
○ Show a caption overlay using position: absolute and opacity.
● Ensure that the gallery is responsive and adjusts image count per row based on screen
width.
● Use CSS box-shadow and border-radius to make images look clean and modern.
7. Design a contact page using a semantic <form>.
Requirements:
● Include fields for Name, Email, Subject, and Message.
● Center the form vertically and horizontally using CSS layout techniques.
● Style all inputs with padding, border-radius, and subtle shadows.
● Change the border color of an input when it is focused (:focus selector).
● Use background-color and color CSS properties to give a consistent theme.
● Add spacing between fields using margins and test layout alignment without using
tables.
8. Create a dashboard layout using only HTML and CSS.
Requirements:
● Header section at the top (fixed position).
● Sidebar on the left containing navigation links.
● Main content area on the right with at least two content boxes.
● Use positioning and flexbox to structure the layout.
● Ensure the sidebar height adjusts to full screen height.
● The content boxes should have shadows and be evenly spaced.
● On smaller screens, the sidebar should stack above the content.
9. Build a CSS layout that mimics a newspaper or magazine column structure.
Requirements:
● Create three vertical text columns using column-count and column-gap.
● Add a heading that spans across all columns using column-span: all;.
● Apply custom font styles and justify all text.
● Include at least one image aligned to the left using float.
● Ensure the layout remains clean and readable when resized.
● Add background color to the page and padding around the main content area.
10. Design a complete webpage layout demonstrating mastery of the CSS Box Model and
positioning.
Requirements:
● Header with navigation (sticky at top).
● Hero section with a large background image and centered welcome text.
● About section with two columns (text + image).
● Services section with three boxes in a row.
● Contact section with a simple form.
● Footer with copyright text.
● Use at least:
○ margin, padding, border, and outline properties.
○ Relative, absolute, and fixed positioning in different sections.
○ flexbox or grid for responsive alignment.
○ rgba() colors and gradients.
● Ensure all elements are visually aligned, balanced, and responsive.